WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.800
Life imitates art and art imitates life,&nbsp;
but sometimes, fiction and reality come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.480
a little too close for comfort. From&nbsp;
specific causes of death to predictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:18.520
of national tragedies, no one intended&nbsp;
these episodes of TV to be so haunting.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:25.040
Since news broke on October 28, 2023, of Matthew&nbsp;
Perry's death, "Friends" fans have been reeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.840
over the loss of the man behind the sarcastic and&nbsp;
loveable Chandler Bing. For many, their comfort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:37.120
show now has a dark cloud over it, especially&nbsp;
since two episodes eerily foreshadow his death.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:43.000
In Season 9, Episode 8, "The One with Rachel's&nbsp;
Other Sister," Chandler suggests to Monica that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.600
they use their expensive wedding plates&nbsp;
for Thanksgiving dinner. She's hesitant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.800
but obliges. Though the plates survive the&nbsp;
tense meal, it's Chandler who accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.920
knocks over the entire box of them, shattering&nbsp;
the dishes on the floor. He tells his friends,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.440
"Well, what do you know? I guess&nbsp;
I'll be the one who dies first."

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.520
While intended as a gag, this line&nbsp;
takes on a whole new meaning now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.560
as Perry is indeed the first of the&nbsp;
core six cast members to pass away.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.200
Similarly, in Season 7, Episode&nbsp;
5, "The One with the Engagement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.000
Picture," Chandler struggles to&nbsp;
smile properly for a photo to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.240
announce his and Monica's engagement in&nbsp;
the newspaper. A frustrated Monica says,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.320
"This picture is supposed to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.200
'Geller and Bing to be married,' not,&nbsp;
'Local woman saves drowning moron!'"

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.320
Authorities initially believed Perry&nbsp;
to have died in a drowning incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:40.520
though the exact cause of his death is still&nbsp;
being investigated as of the making of this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.960
Given the circumstances, some fans are calling&nbsp;
for Monica's line to be cut from the episode.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.320
One of the most heartbreaking "Modern&nbsp;
Family" storylines comes in Season 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:57.200
Episode 11 "Legacy," when Ty Burrell's Phil Dunphy&nbsp;
grows concerned about his aging father Frank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.440
when he's discovered seemingly lost in&nbsp;
the grocery store. Frank, played by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.240
legendary Fred Willard, assures his son that he's&nbsp;
fine, and they spend an enjoyable day together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:12.720
Little does Phil know, it's their final day&nbsp;
together, and Frank passes away soon after.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.840
The episode aired on January 15,&nbsp;
2020, and, exactly four months later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.720
Willard died at the age of 86. Just as&nbsp;
Phil and Frank spent the latter's last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.120
moments reflecting on their relationship&nbsp;
and ending the day on a hopeful note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.480
Burrell and Willard did the same during their&nbsp;
last moments together on the "Modern Family" set.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.680
Frank knew his time was coming to an end,&nbsp;
and Burrell believes Willard did too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.880
even while filming the death of&nbsp;
his "Modern Family" character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.040
Burrell said in a tribute to Willard&nbsp;
published in Entertainment Weekly,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.440
"The reason that moment was so poignant&nbsp;
was that he knew his time was limited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.640
and the only real way for him to say goodbye was&nbsp;
to say, 'I'll see you on the next one.' That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:59.240
the way he always thought. He said it with a&nbsp;
wink, and he knew that that wasn't the case."

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:05.880
In 2015, the Season 1 finale of "Mr. Robot" was&nbsp;
postponed from airing on USA Network because one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.560
scene is darkly similar to a devastating killing&nbsp;
in Roanoke, Virginia, the week before its original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:17.440
release date. The episode shows James Plouffe,&nbsp;
executive vice president of technology for E Corp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.280
shooting himself on national television due&nbsp;
to the irreversibility of the Five/Nine Hack.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.680
Just days before the "Mr. Robot" finale was&nbsp;
due to air, former WDBJ reporter Vester Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:36.280
Flanagan II shot and killed two of his past&nbsp;
colleagues — reporter Alison Parker, 24,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.800
and cameraman Adam Ward, 27, — while&nbsp;
they were live on the air covering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:46.080
the 50th anniversary of Smith Mountain&nbsp;
Lake. Flanagan, who also shot himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.680
was previously fired from WDBJ for&nbsp;
problematic behavior. He had sued the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:57.840
station for discriminating against him, but his&nbsp;
lawsuit was ruled as baseless, ABC News reported.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.680
Ultimately, the "Mr. Robot" finale was postponed&nbsp;
out of sympathy and respect for the victims'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:09.000
loved ones, since the events that unfold in it are&nbsp;
disturbingly reminiscent of the real-life events.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.200
Throughout the run of "Scrubs," the hospital's&nbsp;
unnamed janitor makes the life of the lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.640
J.D., as difficult as possible. He's&nbsp;
always pulling pranks, annoying him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.920
and bending the truth. However, one comment&nbsp;
made by Janitor became eerily factual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.480
four years after it was uttered on "Scrubs."

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:30.400
In Season 6, Episode 7, Iraq veteran&nbsp;
Brian Dancer comes to Sacred Heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.480
suffering memory loss due to a head injury.&nbsp;
As a result, the war becomes a major topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:40.680
of conversation among the staff. At the&nbsp;
end of the episode, Janitor tells J.D.,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.960
"In my opinion, we should be&nbsp;
looking for bin Laden in Pakistan."

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:49.960
In May 2011, four years after the episode aired&nbsp;
and nearly a decade after al-Qaeda leader Osama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:54.880
bin Laden orchestrated the terrorist attack&nbsp;
on New York City's World Trade Center towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.760
U.S. Navy SEALs located him in Pakistan,&nbsp;
where he was swiftly executed. So maybe not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.160
everything that came out of Janitor's&nbsp;
mouth was a downright lie after all.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.640
The short-lived "X-Files" spinoff "The Lone&nbsp;
Gunmen" isn't all that well remembered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.440
including its seeming prediction of the terrorist&nbsp;
attack that changed the United States forever. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:20.600
its pilot episode, which aired on March 4, 2001,&nbsp;
a hacker — hired by powerful government officials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:26.480
— takes control of a Boeing aircraft intending to&nbsp;
fly it into one of the World Trade Center towers.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.000
Though the Lone Gunmen manage to stop the plane,&nbsp;
and the terrorist involved is a hacker instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.520
of hijackers, the show's events are uncannily&nbsp;
similar. In fact, conspiracy theories continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.640
to circle that the real attack was&nbsp;
orchestrated by the U.S. government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.120
just like on the show. Despite this unintentional&nbsp;
prediction, right down to the brand of the plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:51.840
not many people made the connection between the&nbsp;
episode and the September 11, 2001, attacks.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.160
"The Lone Gunmen" executive producer&nbsp;
Frank Spotnitz told The Daily Star,

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.760
"I woke up on September 11 and saw it on TV&nbsp;
and the first thing I thought of was 'The Lone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.440
Gunmen.' But then in the weeks and months that&nbsp;
followed, almost no one noticed the connection."

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.640
Despite running for only 21&nbsp;
episodes between 1987 and 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:16.960
"Second Chance" – later titled "Boys&nbsp;
Will Be Boys" — makes an eerily spot-on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.680
prediction about the eventual death of the&nbsp;
Libyan dictator, Colonel Muammar Gaddafi.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:28.800
Set in the then-futuristic year of 2011, Saint&nbsp;
Peter greets newly deceased souls and has them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.360
take a test to determine if they'll be spending&nbsp;
eternity in heaven or hell. In one episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.120
Gaddafi appears before Peter, in disbelief&nbsp;
that he's dead. When asked to step onto a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.840
machine to decide his fate, it&nbsp;
lights up red. Peter tells him,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.600
"Because you have ordered the deaths of&nbsp;
innocent people by means of terrorist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.160
explosives, you are sentenced to&nbsp;
spend eternity wired as a human bomb."

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.960
Peter's comment likely refers to the April&nbsp;
5, 1986 bombing of a West Berlin dance hall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.760
where two were killed and over&nbsp;
200 were injured. As predicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:07.960
in "Second Chance," Gaddafi died in&nbsp;
2011. Aided by the U.S., Britain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:13.800
and France, he was killed at the hands of his&nbsp;
own people, who had enough of his lengthy regime.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.280
In June 2005, a mere month before four terrorists&nbsp;
bombed the London Underground and a bus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.360
the cast of "Spooks" — a British drama about&nbsp;
security intelligence agents working to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.720
threats — shot an episode that involved an&nbsp;
attempted bombing on the city's transportation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:34.160
network. Though the fictional agents stopped the&nbsp;
attack, this wasn't the case in the real-life July&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:42.040
7 event, which killed 52 and injured over 770.&nbsp;
"Spooks" star Peter Firth told The Independent,

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.560
"At one point, the episode wasn't going&nbsp;
to be shown because it was too near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.520
the mark. In the end, the episode went&nbsp;
out in a very heavily edited version."

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:56.600
The amended episode eventually aired in September&nbsp;
2005, just a few months after the real bombing.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.440
The show's ability to predict the&nbsp;
future wasn't limited to this instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.720
with another example including Russian tensions&nbsp;
with the U.K. Richard Armitage, who played agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:10.760
Lucas North, was a little freaked out over&nbsp;
the show's preemptive plotlines, commenting,

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.320
"Are the 'Spooks' team writing&nbsp;
the newspapers as well?"

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.040
"Legends of Chamberlain Heights" was&nbsp;
a Comedy Central cartoon that followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:24.040
three aspiring young basketball players,&nbsp;
running from 2016 to 2017. In one episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:29.280
the trio of Grover, Malik, and Jamal&nbsp;
encounter the legendary Kobe Bryant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.760
after a helicopter crash. He's on the ground&nbsp;
being crushed by the chopper while clutching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.440
his trophies and asking the boys to help him.&nbsp;
Before they can act, the helicopter explodes.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.880
In January 2020, Bryant died tragically in a&nbsp;
helicopter crash along with his 13-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.440
daughter Gianna. Overcome by thick fog, the&nbsp;
pilot was said to have lost his sense of spatial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:55.880
awareness. This caused him to unintentionally&nbsp;
fly downward and crash into a hillside, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:02.400
the helicopter exploded into flames. None of the&nbsp;
nine individuals on board survived the incident.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:08.200
Captain James T. Kirk and Science Officer Mr.&nbsp;
Spock are rather stressed in "Star Trek" Season 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:12.720
Episode 26, "Assignment: Earth,"&nbsp;
because interstellar agent Gary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.200
Seven threatens to wreak havoc on&nbsp;
the events of the 20th century. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:21.760
particularly eerie moment comes when&nbsp;
Spock talks about an assassination.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.080
"Current Earth crises would fill a tape bank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.760
Captain. There will be an&nbsp;
important assassination today."

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:34.000
This episode aired on March 29, 1968,&nbsp;
and only a few days later, on April 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.160
civil rights activist Martin Luther King Jr. was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:42.040
shot and killed on his balcony at the&nbsp;
Lorraine Motel in Memphis, Tennessee.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:46.440
King was actually a massive fan of "Star&nbsp;
Trek," and he was the one who encouraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.000
actress Nichelle Nichols — one of&nbsp;
the first black women to play such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.720
a powerful character on screen — to stay on&nbsp;
the show as Lt. Uhura after she contemplated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.800
leaving TV for Broadway. Nichols said in&nbsp;
a Reddit AMA thread that King told her,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.720
"If you leave, they can replace you with a&nbsp;
blonde-haired white girl, and it will be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.000
you were never there. What you've accomplished,&nbsp;
for all of us, will only be real if you stay."

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.600
On November 7, 2001, less than two months after&nbsp;
the attack on the Twin Towers in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.680
"South Park" creators Trey Parker&nbsp;
and Matt Stone did what they do best:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.480
concoct an over-the-top episode about a headline.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.320
In Season 5, Episode 9, the boys travel to&nbsp;
Afghanistan to return a goat sent to them by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:33.240
four Afghan children as reciprocation for the $1&nbsp;
their teacher forced them to donate. While there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.000
Cartman and friends are taken hostage by bin Laden&nbsp;
and the Taliban. After a "Looney Tunes"-esque&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.120
segment between Cartman and the militant,&nbsp;
bin Laden finds himself donning an Uncle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:47.720
Sam ensemble. Not knowing who he is, bin Laden's&nbsp;
followers fire at him, causing the explosive in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:52.720
his hand to detonate. A U.S. Navy SEAL then&nbsp;
delivers one more shot to bin Laden's head.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.520
To put it lightly, this is not how&nbsp;
things went down in real life. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:01.480
Parker and Stone got a few things right. He&nbsp;
was secretly living in plain view of everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:06.640
though in Pakistan, not Afghanistan. And,&nbsp;
of course, he was shot and killed by a SEAL.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:11.080
In Season 10, Episode 22 of "Family&nbsp;
Guy," several tales are told,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:15.360
including one where Peter Griffin gains&nbsp;
the ability to turn everyone in Quahog into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.680
Robin Williams. Though it's fun at first,&nbsp;
Peter soon becomes agitated about being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:24.840
surrounded by endless versions of the actor.&nbsp;
He even tries to kill himself to escape them.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:30.920
The episode originally aired in May 2012, but&nbsp;
reruns were shown for years after. On August 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:36.400
2014, the episode finished airing on&nbsp;
BBC Three in the U.K. just as the news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:41.520
broke of Williams' death by suicide. A&nbsp;
BBC spokesperson told The Independent,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.320
"This was a repeat that we have shown a&nbsp;
couple of times before, so who could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:50.600
planned that? It was scheduled more than two&nbsp;
weeks ago, it is just an uncanny coincidence."

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:55.240
Another "Family Guy" episode came extremely close&nbsp;
to predicting the death of U.S. Supreme Court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:01.360
Justice Antonin Scalia. In Season 5, Episode&nbsp;
18, Peter calls on Death to send him back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:07.200
time so that he can stop his 18-year-old self from&nbsp;
canceling a date with his now-wife Lois. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:11.960
Peter accidentally changes the past,&nbsp;
and Lois ends up with Glenn Quagmire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:16.640
When Peter asks Death to send him back so&nbsp;
he can make things right, Death recounts,

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.200
"Dick Cheney, the chairman of Haliburton,&nbsp;
shot Supreme Court Justice Scalia in a hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:26.080
accident and the bullet went right through&nbsp;
him and killed Karl Rove and Tucker Carlson."

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.200
While that exact outlandish&nbsp;
situation did not happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:33.920
Scalia did die while on a hunting&nbsp;
getaway in Texas. On February 13,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:40.000
2016, about a decade after the 2007 "Family&nbsp;
Guy" episode aired, Scalia was found dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:45.240
by ranch owner John Poindexter after the&nbsp;
Associate Justice didn't attend breakfast.

